P5.4 — long audio: what a real-length recitation costs transcribeSession
specs/readiness-recovery-10-10/tasks.md P5.4

date (UTC): 2026-08-03
measured by: scripts/measure-long-audio.mjs, against a loopback ASR (no torch, no model download)
             run with `node --expose-gc scripts/measure-long-audio.mjs`

  minutes  chunks  audio    elapsed  peak RSS  worst event-loop stall
     1       600   1.9MB     0.03s      24MB       16ms
     5      3000   9.6MB     0.12s      99MB      102ms
    15      9000  28.8MB     0.39s     302MB      356ms

TWO CEILINGS, BOTH LINEAR IN SESSION LENGTH

  1. Memory is roughly 10x the audio. 28.8MB of PCM becomes ~302MB RSS: a readFileSync per chunk,
     one Buffer.concat over the whole session, then a base64 string of the lot. A 30-minute
     recitation would be ~600MB for a SINGLE learner's finalize.

  2. The event loop stalls ~24ms per audio-minute. Note it is not SLOW — 0.39s for fifteen minutes
     of audio is fine. It is BLOCKING: the reads and the concat are synchronous, so for those 356ms
     every other learner's analysis request waits. ~700ms at 30 minutes, ~1.4s at an hour.

  The second is the one that matters, because the cost does not land on the session that caused it.
  One learner finishing a long memorization session degrades everybody.

HOW MUCH THIS MATTERS DEPENDS ON SESSION LENGTH, WHICH NOBODY HAS SET
  Al-Fatihah is ~30 seconds; at that length this is invisible. Memorization practice is plausibly
  5-30 minutes. There is no product answer yet for how long a session may be, and no SLO to measure
  against (P5.1 is open), so these are measurements without a target rather than a pass or a fail.

NOT FIXED. The direction is to stream chunks to the ASR instead of materialising the session —
chunked transfer, or an upload the ASR reads incrementally. That is a refactor of transcribeSession
AND of the ASR contract, not a patch, and it should be done against a length target somebody has
actually chosen.

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
FIXED — 2026-08-04. Re-measured with the same script, same machine, same loopback ASR.

  minutes  chunks  audio    elapsed  peak RSS  worst event-loop stall
     1       600   1.9MB     0.04s      28MB        0ms   (was  16ms)
     5      3000   9.6MB     0.13s     106MB       22ms   (was 102ms)
    15      9000  28.8MB     0.39s     291MB       80ms   (was 356ms)

CEILING 2 — the one that mattered — is 4.4x better at fifteen minutes: ~24ms per audio-minute down
to ~5ms. Elapsed is unchanged, which is the point: this was never a speed problem. The assembly now
reads asynchronously in batches of 64, so the loop stays free to serve other learners while one
person's long session is being put together.

CEILING 1 — memory — is UNCHANGED at ~10x the audio (302MB -> 291MB is noise). Removing it needs the
whole session to stop existing as one buffer, which means streaming the upload to the ASR service.
That service takes `audioBase64` inside a JSON body, so it is a two-service contract change, not a
refactor. Still open, still linear, still ~600MB for a thirty-minute recitation.

AND A GUESS THAT WAS WRONG, KEPT BECAUSE IT WAS EXPENSIVE TO HAVE

The obvious next move looked like the base64 conversion: 28.8MB of PCM becomes a 38.4MB string in
one synchronous call. A chunked version was written (slicing on multiples of 3 so the output is
byte-identical) and measured: 80ms -> 78ms. Nothing. It was reverted rather than kept.

Timed directly, on the 15-minute buffer:

    Buffer.concat   2.7ms
    wav framing     2.0ms
    base64          2.8ms   -> 38.4MB string

Seven and a half milliseconds of the remaining eighty. The single big allocation is NOT where the
cost is, despite being the thing that looks expensive. Whatever is left is spread across the 9000
reads and their JSON.parse, not concentrated in the one place intuition points at.

Recorded so the next person does not spend the same afternoon optimising base64.
